Packet

Header layout and flag bits. For what packets are for and how they are
routed, see upstream docs/source/understanding.rst#understanding-transport
and #understanding-packetformat.

Vectors: every kind whose raw is a whole packet, that is
test/announce/, test/plain/, test/pathrequest/, test/encrypted/,
test/linkrequest/, test/linkproof/, test/linkdata/, test/proof/ and
test/ifac/.


Constants

    MTU               500 bytes    RNS/Reticulum.py#MTU
    HEADER_MAXSIZE     35 bytes    RNS/Reticulum.py#HEADER_MAXSIZE
    IFAC_MIN_SIZE       1 byte     RNS/Reticulum.py#IFAC_MIN_SIZE
    MDU               464 bytes    RNS/Reticulum.py#MDU
    PATHFINDER_M      128 hops     RNS/Transport.py#PATHFINDER_M


Header

Header 1, the normal form:

    offset  size  field
    0       1     flags
    1       1     hops
    2       16    destination hash
    18      1     context
    19      -     payload

Header 2, used while a packet is in transport:

    offset  size  field
    0       1     flags
    1       1     hops
    2       16    transport id
    18      16    destination hash
    34      1     context
    35      -     payload

The transport id is inserted before the destination hash, not after.

    RNS/Packet.py#pack, RNS/Packet.py#unpack

The context byte names what the payload holds, and which names exist
depends on the packet type; doc/fields lists every one this format has
a name for. It has a name only where a vector carries that byte, so
values the reference defines and no vector uses print as two hex digits
rather than as a keyword. test/plain/unnamed-context carries
CACHE_REQUEST for that reason.

    RNS/Packet.py#CACHE_REQUEST


Flags

One byte at offset 0.

    bit  width  field
    7    1      interface access code present
    6    1      header type
    5    1      context flag
    4    1      transport type
    3-2  2      destination type
    1-0  2      packet type

    header type        0  header 1        1  header 2
    transport type     0  broadcast       1  transport
    destination type   0  single          1  group
                       2  plain           3  link
    packet type        0  data            1  announce
                       2  linkrequest     3  proof

The header type bit encodes header 1 as zero. Vectors print the
human-facing numbering, so bit 6 clear appears as header_type 1.

The transport type is one bit and holds two of the four values
RNS/Transport.py#types defines. RELAY and TUNNEL do not survive a pack:
get_packed_flags shifts the value left by four, so 2 lands on the
context flag and 3 on the context flag and the transport bit together,
and unpack reads the bit back as broadcast or transport either way.
There is no vector for them because there are no bytes to record.

    RNS/Transport.py#RELAY, RNS/Transport.py#TUNNEL

The context flag has no fixed meaning. Its interpretation depends on
the packet type; for announces it signals the presence of a ratchet.

Bit 7 is not read by Packet.unpack, which is why every other field
above is described from RNS/Packet.py#header_type and this one is
not. It is set and cleared a layer below, and a frame that carries it
is not a packet until it has been unmasked. See the section below.

The destination type is read from the destination the packet is
addressed to, with one exception: a link request proof writes it as
link from a constant. See doc/link.

    RNS/Packet.py#get_packed_flags, RNS/Packet.py#hops


Hops

One byte at offset 1. A packet with hops greater than or equal to
PATHFINDER_M, that is 128, is rejected before any further parsing.

Two vectors carry the boundary, because one cannot tell a wrong
threshold from a missing one:

    test/announce/hop-limit         128 hops, rejected
    test/announce/hop-limit-below   127 hops, decodes

The hop count is not part of any signed material, so the second vector
verifies exactly as test/announce/minimal does.

    RNS/Packet.py#PATHFINDER_M


Entering transport

A transport node rewriting a header 1 packet into a header 2 packet
performs exactly this transformation:

    flags = old_flags | (1 << 6) | (transport_type << 4)
    out   = flags || hops || transport_id || old[2:]

The flags byte is combined with a bitwise or, not replaced. The rest
of the original packet from offset 2 onward, that is the destination
hash, the context byte and the payload, is copied unchanged. The
payload is not re-signed, and for an announce the signature therefore
still validates.

Three vectors carry the transformation, and they are three because the
transformation is only interesting where something is hashed:

    test/announce/in-transport         announce/minimal, one hop
    test/linkrequest/in-transport      linkrequest/signalling, one hop
    test/proof/proved-in-transport     the proved packet of proof/implicit,
                                       three hops, with the proof unchanged

An announce derives no link id and no packet hash, so the first says
nothing about the branch that drops the transport id before hashing.
The other two do. The link request opens the same link as the request
it was made from, and the proof still addresses the right packet and
still verifies, over a packet whose bytes have changed. That is what
excluding the low four flag bits, the hop count and the transport id
buys, and it is the whole reason for the rule.

    RNS/Transport.py#HEADER_2


Proofs

Packet type 3, destination type single, context 0x00, unencrypted.

A receiver that accepts a data packet answers with a proof. The address
field holds no destination hash: it is the first 16 bytes of the proved
packet's hash, which is how the sender recognises the answer to its own
packet and why a proof needs no destination of its own.

    RNS/Packet.py#get_hash

The packet hash is taken over the whole packet with the low four flag
bits and the hop count excluded, so that it survives a hop. It is the
same material a link id is taken over, untrimmed and not truncated.

    RNS/Packet.py#get_hash, RNS/Packet.py#get_hashable_part

Two payload lengths decode:

    64    implicit    signature over the packet hash
    96    explicit    packet hash, 32 bytes, then the signature

The reference emits the implicit form: use_implicit_proof defaults to
true and a configuration may turn it off. It accepts either form.

    RNS/Reticulum.py#__use_implicit_proof, RNS/Packet.py#EXPL_LENGTH

The explicit form carries a hash the receiver already computed, so the
extra 32 bytes prove nothing on their own. A decoder that trusts them
instead of the hash it computed will accept a proof for a packet it
never sent.

The signature is over the packet hash, never over the proof packet.

test/proof/implicit and test/proof/explicit carry the two forms of the
same proof, so their signatures are identical.

A proof therefore makes two claims. The signature says who accepted the
packet; the address says which packet, and the reference reads the two
in different places. On a link the address is the whole of the match:
the proof goes to the link whose id it carries and to no other. To a
destination it is what a transport node looks up in its reverse table
to send the proof back the way the packet came, while the end that sent
the packet matches an explicit proof by the hash in its payload and
tries an implicit one against every outstanding receipt in turn.

    RNS/Transport.py#reverse_table, RNS/Packet.py#validate_proof

So a decoder that never computes the address still concludes something,
by exhaustion, and a transport node that never computes it drops every
proof it should have relayed. Three derived vectors break one claim
each, and the two that leave the signature alone are the ones a reader
is likeliest to pass by accident:

    test/proof/altered-signature      addressed correctly; the
                                      signature does not verify
    test/proof/destination-mismatch   the signature verifies; the
                                      address is one byte off
    test/proof/link-id-mismatch       the same on a link, where the
                                      address is the link id

A proof for a packet on a link is the same packet type addressed
differently and signed by a different key. See doc/link.

    RNS/Identity.py#prove


Plain destinations

Destination type 2. The payload is the data: Destination.encrypt
returns the plaintext unchanged for this type, so there is no ephemeral
key, no token and no padding, and the minimum payload is nothing at
all.

    RNS/Destination.py#PLAIN

    offset  size  field
    19      -     the data, unencrypted

A plain destination holds no keys and has no identity, so its hash is
SHA-256 of the name hash alone. See doc/destination.

A path request is addressed to one, so a transport node reads a plain
packet whether or not it ever encrypts anything. Its payload is the
section below.

test/plain/payload carries 26 bytes that mean nothing, which is the
point: a plain destination hands the payload through whatever it holds.
test/plain/empty carries no payload at all. A packet to a single
destination cannot: PKCS7 adds a full block to an empty plaintext, and
the token adds 48 bytes around it. See doc/encryption.


Vector format: plain

kind: plain, determinism: encode

raw is one line:

    line 1    the complete packet

expect names the header fields, the payload length and the payload.
There is no key anywhere in the vector, because there is nothing to
decrypt.


Path requests

A plain packet addressed to the destination named

    rnstransport.path.request

carries two or three fields, concatenated with no count, no type byte
and no delimiter:

    offset  size  field
    19      16    the destination hash being asked after
    35      16    the transport id of the requesting instance, or absent
    35/51   -     the tag, or absent

Which of the three are present is decided by the payload length alone:

    length       reading
    below 16     nothing is read; the request is dropped
    16           destination only, no tag; the request is dropped
    17 to 32     destination, then the tag
    above 32     destination, transport id, then the tag

    RNS/Transport.py#transport_enabled,
    RNS/Transport.py#TRUNCATED_HASHLENGTH

The sender writes the transport id when the instance is itself a
transport node and omits it otherwise, but the reader cannot tell the
two apart except by length. A 17-byte tag written without a transport
id is read as a transport id followed by a tag of one byte.
test/pathrequest/ambiguous is that packet. There is no way to decode it
correctly, because there is no correct decoding: the reference reads it
the same way, and an implementation that reads it any other way has
disagreed with the network.

At most 16 bytes of the tag reach the duplicate check:

    unique_tag = destination_hash || tag[0:16]

Two requests for the same destination whose tags agree in their first
16 bytes are one request, and the second is dropped.
test/pathrequest/long-tag carries a tag of 17 bytes after the split and
records that only 16 of them count.

A request with no tag at all is read in full and then dropped, which is
why test/pathrequest/tagless decodes and carries accepted no rather
than a rejection.


Vector format: pathrequest

kind: pathrequest, determinism: encode

raw is one line:

    line 1    the complete packet

expect names the header fields, then the three payload fields, the
unique tag derived from two of them, and whether the reference acts on
the request. A payload shorter than the destination hash it is supposed
to name is a rejection:

    invalid            short-payload      test/pathrequest/short
    payload_length     15
    minimum_length     16

The handler has no rule of its own for that length; it reads nothing
and returns. The corpus names the length that failed.


Interface access codes

For what these are for, see upstream
docs/source/understanding.rst#understanding-protocolspecifics, section
"Interface Access Codes". What follows is where the bytes go.

An interface configured with a named network or a passphrase carries
every packet inside a frame:

    offset  size  field
    0       1     flags, bit 7 set, masked
    1       1     hops, masked
    2       N     access code, not masked
    2+N     -     the packet from offset 2 onward, masked

N is configuration, not a field. It is not on the wire and both ends
must already agree on it. The default is 16 for the TCP interfaces
(RNS/Interfaces/TCPInterface.py#DEFAULT_IFAC_SIZE) and a
configuration may set any size at or above IFAC_MIN_SIZE.

The access code is the last N bytes of an Ed25519 signature over the
unframed packet, made with an identity both ends derive from the
network name and passphrase:

    origin      full_hash(netname) || full_hash(netkey)
    key         hkdf(64, full_hash(origin), salt = IFAC_SALT)
    identity    Identity.from_bytes(key)
    code        identity.sign(packet)[-N:]

Either half of origin is omitted when that half is not configured, and
at least one must be present or the interface has no access code at
all. Nothing on the wire says which of the three shapes was used, so
the three are three vectors:

    test/ifac/announce          a network name alone
    test/ifac/passphrase        both, name first
    test/ifac/passphrase-only   a passphrase alone

The middle one is where the order can be got wrong, and the other two
are what keeps a swap from looking like a different configuration.

IFAC_SALT is a constant, RNS/Reticulum.py#IFAC_SALT.

The mask is hkdf over the frame length, keyed by the access code and
salted with the interface key:

    mask        hkdf(len(frame), derive_from = code, salt = key)

Every byte is xored with it except the access code itself, which has
to be readable before the mask it keys can be generated. Both header
bytes are masked, and the sender puts bit 7 back afterwards, so the
flag survives a mask that would have cleared it.

    RNS/Transport.py#ifac_identity, RNS/Transport.py#masked_raw,
    RNS/Transport.py#unmasked_raw

A decoder that treats bit 7 as reserved reads a masked frame as a
packet: random flags, a random hop count, and a destination hash that
matches nothing.

Every ifac vector carries test/announce/minimal inside such a frame,
and the four the reference produced differ only in the configuration
that keys it. test/ifac/minimum-size is the same packet with a one-byte
access code, which is IFAC_MIN_SIZE: the size changes where the packet
starts and how much of the frame is masked, and nothing in the frame
announces either.

The fifth, test/ifac/altered-frame, has one bit of the last frame byte
flipped. The mask is a keystream and the access code is not altered, so
the same bit is flipped in the packet that comes out and the code
covers a packet that is no longer there. It is the only vector where
ifac_valid is no.


Vector format

kind: announce, determinism: encode

raw is one line:

    line 1    the complete packet

A packet that does decode is encode class: expect names every field the
header and the payload hold, in order, and cmd/check lays them back out
and diffs the result against raw. A packet that broke a rule has no
fields to lay out and is decode class.

A packet that cannot be decoded produces the rule it broke and the
numbers behind it. Each rule has a vector:

    invalid            short-header       test/announce/no-header
    length             1
    minimum_length     2

    invalid            short-header       test/announce/short-header
    length             18
    minimum_length     19

    invalid            short-header       test/announce/short-header-2
    length             34
    minimum_length     35

    invalid            hop-limit          test/announce/hop-limit
    hops               128
    hop_limit          128

    invalid            short-payload      test/announce/short-payload
    payload_length     147
    minimum_length     148

short-header has three thresholds and all three are on file. Two of
them are the length of a header, 19 for a header 1 and 35 for a header
2, and which applies follows from bit 6 rather than from a constant. A
decoder that takes 19 for granted reads 34 bytes of a header 2 as a
whole packet and takes the transport id for a destination hash. The
third is the two bytes it takes to read that bit at all, and it is the
only threshold in the format that is not a length of something.

See doc/fields for every name that can appear in a rejection.


What has no vector

Three things a decoder must handle have no vector here, and none of
them is an oversight. A vector records what the reference produced, so
a case the reference cannot be made to produce has no bytes to record.
Passing the corpus says nothing about any of the three.

What puts a case here is that no reference function can be made to
write the bytes. It is not that the layers above the packet do not
build the payload: what a link carries is chosen by whoever sends it,
and a plaintext chosen here and handed to Link.encrypt and Packet.pack
is the reference's output as much as any other. 15 vectors are built
that way and each says so in its source, which names the chosen
plaintext before it names the pin.

Nor is it that one function on the way writes something else. A
function can be replaced for the call that writes a vector, and four
tokens are, so what remains is whether every other byte is still the
reference's. See doc/encryption, section "Padding the writer does not
write".

    a header that does not parse, in any kind but announce

        Packet.unpack is one function and the header does not vary
        with the kind around it, so the four announce rejections above
        are the rule and not a sample of it. Repeating them per kind
        would be a table of permutations. RNS/Packet.py#unpack.

    a context byte the reference defines and no object sets

        0xfd is LINKPROOF and nothing sets it; see doc/link. Others
        are defined and unused, and a context with no name prints as
        two hex digits. test/plain/unnamed-context is the vector for
        that behaviour rather than for any one of the values.

    a transport type of relay or tunnel

        One bit holds two of the four values RNS/Transport.py#types
        defines. See the Flags section above.

Building any of the three means composing bytes the reference did not
write, which is the one thing raw does not hold. See CLAUDE.md.


Vector format: proof

kind: proof, determinism: encode

raw is three lines:

    line 1    the packet being proved
    line 2    the public key of the identity that signed, 64 bytes
    line 3    the proof packet

Neither of the first two is on the wire with the proof. The packet
hash is in no packet, and the key belongs to the destination the proved
packet was addressed to, which the sender already holds and a decoder
here is handed.

expect names both, the header fields, which form the proof takes, the
hash computed from line 1, the hash the proof carries if it carries
one, the address the proof should have, and the verdict.


Vector format: ifac

kind: ifac, determinism: encode

raw is four lines:

    line 1    the network name, utf-8 bytes, or - if not configured
    line 2    the passphrase, utf-8 bytes, or - if not configured
    line 3    the access code size, one byte
    line 4    the frame as it appears on the wire

The first three are configuration rather than fields of the packet. A
receiver is told all three; nothing on the wire says any of them.

expect names them, the origin, the key derived from it, the frame
length, the access code the frame carries, the packet recovered from
it, the access code that packet should have produced, and whether the
two agree.

The frame is what the reference produced: tools/gen hands the packet to
Transport.transmit and writes down what came back. The key is not. RNS
derives it inline in the constructor of a running instance and exposes
no function to call, so tools/gen composes it from the same three RNS
functions in the same order.

    RNS/Reticulum.py#ifac_netname to :971

Encode class: cmd/check masks the packet again and diffs the frame.
The corpus therefore checks both directions of the mask, which is the
one place it does so with the sender's code path rather than the
receiver's.
